# OKS-602 — release image: packages a binary already built natively by the
# GitHub release workflow (one job per arch, no QEMU cross-compilation), then
# merged into a multi-arch manifest. The plain `Dockerfile` builds from
# source and stays the local/dev path.
#
# OKS-689 — runtime is distroless instead of debian:bookworm-slim. On the fleet
# image the same swap removed all 56 CVE findings, criticals included (perl-base
# x3 and zlib1g, none of which Debian has a fix for, in bookworm or in trixie),
# for packages this agent never touches.
#
# glibc compatibility is unchanged by the move: distroless/cc-debian12 is
# bookworm underneath, so it is the same glibc 2.36 this image already ran on
# while the release workflow builds on ubuntu-24.04 runners. If a future
# toolchain bump starts emitting newer symbol versions, this breaks exactly the
# way debian:bookworm-slim already would — see the 0.23.2 "GLIBC_2.38 not
# found" release.
#
# The base ships no zlib. That is fine because Cargo.toml selects mysql_async's
# `minimal-rust` feature (flate2's pure-Rust backend) rather than `minimal`,
# which despite the name links C zlib. A binary built from a tree predating that
# change will fail here with "libz.so.1: cannot open shared object file".
FROM gcr.io/distroless/cc-debian12
ARG TARGETARCH
ARG APP_VERSION=""
ENV APP_VERSION=$APP_VERSION
COPY dist/okstatus-agent-linux-${TARGETARCH} /usr/local/bin/okstatus-agent
# Numeric form: distroless has no useradd, and the binary needs no passwd entry.
USER 1001:1001
EXPOSE 9091
# Absolute path — distroless has no shell for a PATH lookup.
CMD ["/usr/local/bin/okstatus-agent"]
